problems.problem:
Find all positive integers $n$ which satisfy the following two conditions:
(a) $n$ has at least four different positive divisors;
(b) for any divisors $a$ and $b$ of $n$ satisfying $1<a<b<n$, the number $b-a$ divides $n$.

Three strictly increasing sequences $$a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots,\qquad b_1, b_2, b_3, \ldots,\qquad c_1, c_2, c_3, \ldots$$ of positive integers are given. Every positive integer belongs to exactly one of the three sequences. For every positive integer $n$, the following conditions hold:
(a) $c_{a_n}=b_n+1$;
(b) $a_{n+1}>b_n$;
(c) the number $c_{n+1}c_n-(n+1)c_{n+1}-nc_n$ is even.
Find $a_{2010}$, $b_{2010}$ and $c_{2010}$.

We are given a positive integer $n$ which is not a power of two. Show that there exists a positive integer $m$ with the following two properties:
(a) $m$ is the product of two consecutive positive integers;
(b) the decimal representation of $m$ consists of two identical blocks with $n$ digits.
